`ClassInfo` is now built by `object.ClassInfoOf`

Previously the compiler generated the `ClassInfo` of every class and interface
(the `TypeInfo_Class` instance that `.classinfo` refers to) with a layout fixed in
the compiler, and required `object.d` to declare `TypeInfo_Class` with exactly
that layout.

Now, for every class and interface `T`, the compiler evaluates
`object.ClassInfoOf!T()` at compile time and emits the `TypeInfo_Class` instance
it returns. druntime's `ClassInfoOf` produces the same `ClassInfo` as before.

A runtime that wants a different `ClassInfo` declares its own `TypeInfo_Class`
and `ClassInfoOf`:

---
class TypeInfo_Class : TypeInfo
{
    TypeInfo_Class base;
    void* destructor;
    ubyte numInterfaces;    // the Interface[] array follows the ClassInfo
}

TypeInfo_Class ClassInfoOf(T)()
{
    auto ci = new TypeInfo_Class;
    static if (is(T S == super) && S.length && is(S[0] == class))
        ci.base = S[0].classinfo;
    static if (__traits(hasMember, T, "__xdtor") && __traits(isSame, __traits(parent, T.__xdtor), T))
        ci.destructor = cast(void*) &T.__xdtor;
    ci.numInterfaces = cast(ubyte) __traits(getInterfaces, T).length;
    return ci;
}
---

The `Interface[]` array and the interface vtables are still generated by the
compiler, directly after the instance. The instance must therefore be a
`TypeInfo_Class`, not a subclass of it. If `object.d` does not declare
`ClassInfoOf`, no `ClassInfo` is generated and the first vtable entry is null.
